{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at 183ebe7d00f8821ea79875b42bb7cb265f6efdf1. limits.md documents the shipped sequencer queue bound, and says what the back-pressure sentinel does not promise.\n\nWHAT THE PAGE NOW SAYS. A Concurrent submissions section: the resident admits 32 submissions into the sequencer at once, counting the one holding the lock, so 32 means one in progress and 31 waiting. Beyond that a submission is refused rather than queued, with \"sequencer at capacity\", taken before the signed intent is parsed, before admission hooks, before the payload tree is written and before anything is chained onto the sequence ref. The bound belongs to the resident; a program embedding the kernel directly may leave it unset and get an unbounded queue.\n\nTHE PARAGRAPH I ADDED THAT THE ORIGINAL DRAFT DID NOT HAVE. ErrBackPressure is returned from two places, not one: the capacity refusal, and an exhausted retry limit while chaining under contention. Sharing a sentinel is deliberate — both mean \"overload, try again later\", and errors.Is is what separates overload from a malformed or unauthorized submission, which is the distinction a caller actually needs. But only the capacity refusal is free. A retry exhaustion has already decoded the intent and written objects.\n\nThe draft I re-cut described only the cheap case and asserted that a refused submission costs almost nothing and leaves no object behind. Read against the sentinel as a whole that is false, and it is false in the direction that matters: a caller trusting it would assume a failed submission left the repository untouched. I found this by checking the claim against kernel.go rather than carrying the sentence forward, and the page now states the limit of the guarantee explicitly. The code is unchanged — the dual meaning is documented at kernel.go:30-36 and pinned by TestExhaustedRetryLimitIsBackPressureNotAnonymousFailure, and the request asks to preserve agreed behaviour, so the defect was in the prose and the prose is where it is fixed.\n\nCITATIONS. The page cites the two behaviour artifacts published at the code commit in this same head, 774efb29 at internal/kernel and 158f7dfd at internal/app, alongside what it already carried. It cites no retired artifact; the previous head cited f6c96085, which was retired, and that is the reason this work was re-cut at all.\n\nGATES at this exact head: the docset suite uncached at 95.423s, make docs at 94.947s, git diff --check clean, and merge-tree against main 27603774 clean. The code gates at the commit below it: go vet clean, go build clean, kernel 131.778s, app 36.381s, kernel -race 94.192s.\n\nThis rests on the live non-stale predecessor at this path, b930725a, which stands at main.","body":{"commit":"183ebe7d00f8821ea79875b42bb7cb265f6efdf1","path":"docs"}}